ground_truth:
{
  "summary": "During the first week of January 2025, the most prominent terms in the Bluesky collection were a mixture of very high-frequency general discussion terms and politically focused terms. The strongest topical signals were around US politics, especially Trump, elections, presidency, government, democracy, Republicans/Democrats, law, media, and Elon Musk.",
  "key_points": [
    "The highest-frequency general terms included 'people' (64,583), 'year' (47,874), 'time' (42,216), 'thing' (30,709), 'day' (26,508), 'way' (25,868), and 'thank' (25,153).",
    "The most prominent topical term was 'trump' (47,883), indicating that Trump-related discussion was a major feature of Bluesky discourse during this week. Other strongly political terms included 'country' (17,466), 'president' (14,062), 'state' (12,423), 'maga' (11,271), 'election' (11,141), 'party' (10,530), 'government' (9,911), 'law' (8,982), 'Biden' (8,458), 'Republican' (8,183), 'Democracy' (7,594), and 'Democrat' (7,420).",
    "A second strong theme concerned Elon Musk, social media platforms, and media ecosystems: terms such as 'Musk' (14,419), 'media' (12,747), 'Twitter' (4,601), 'platform' (3,496), 'Tesla' (3,469), and 'Elon' (8,013) .",
    "International politics and geopolitical anxieties also appeared. Terms such as russia, putin, ukrain, gaza, israel, canada, greenland, panama, china, nato, trudeau, and denmark suggest the conversation was not only US-domestic, likely relate to specific early-January news cycles",
    "The returned terms are stemmed, so forms like 'presid', 'elect', 'govern', 'democraci', and 'januari' should be read as normalized versions of president/presidency, election/elect, government/governance, democracy, and January."
  ]
}

{
  "summary": "There are three distinct topic clusters across the two days. December 24 (Two Groupings): The data splits the discussions into two distinct nodes.One is focused on everyday life and holiday preparation (terms like christma, hous, car, famili, shop).The second, much smaller node is heavily focused on geopolitics and the Israel-Hamas conflict (terms like israel, hama, protest, gaza, war).December 25 (One Merged Grouping): By Christmas Day, the algorithm groups the conversation into one single node. -The Connections (Links): The graph uses directed links to connect both of the distinct December 24 nodes to the single December 25 node. This shows that the previously separated conversations from Christmas Eve merged into a shared vocabulary on Christmas Day, combining holiday terms (christma, famili, kid) with conflict terms (hama, war, protest).-Strongest Node (By Volume): The largest overall topic grouping in the dataset is the Holiday & Everyday Life node on December 24 (id: 20231224-0)-Strongest Link (By Weight): The strongest thematic connection flowing between the two dates is the link connecting the December 24 Holiday node to the December 25 Merged node. This edge has a weight of 15, indicating it shares the highest volume of common, overlapping terms across the date boundary.",
  "key_points": ["node1: Dec 24,403 (Large),Everyday Life & Holiday Prep: peopl, year, christma, hous, car, home, dog, famili, shop",
"node2: Dec 24,8 (Small),Geopolitics & Conflict: israel, hama (Hamas), children, protest, war, palestin, gaza",
"node3: Dec 25,51 (Medium),Christmas Day Convergence: christma, famili, kid, protest, covid, children, war, hama"]
}

